{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Now the earth was unformed and void.</b> This is matter that has no substance, and no name apprehends it; it is in the language of the Sages (״כתוהא על הראשונות״) — as if one were to decree upon it any thing, it would be stunned (מתוהא) and reconsider to call it by another name, because it had not put on a form by which a name could apprehend it. And this matter is called in the language of the Torah תהו (unformed), and in the language of the philosophers היולי (hylē). And בהו (void) is the form that is clothed upon the matter, and it is a compound word — בו־הוא — that is, a thing that has in it form. Of this the verse says (Isaiah 34:11): ״ונטה עליה קו תהו ואבני בהו״ — and He stretched over it a line of תהו and stones of בהו; He attributed the line to תהו, which is the matter that has no substance, because the line is what shows the craftsman's conception of the building he hopes to make, and the word is derived from (Psalms 40:2): ״קוה קותי ה׳״ — I have hoped, I have waited for HaShem. And He attributed the stones to בהו, which is the form, because the stones are the form of the building. And what is tied together in these two verses comes together here: at first He created something from nothing, the Master of all powers — the matter of the heavens and the matter of the earth. And the earth, after this creation, was תהו, that is, matter without substance, and it was בהו, for He clothed it with form. And Scripture mentions four elements that are in this form, and they are fire, wind (רוח), water, and dust; and the word הארץ (the earth) includes all four of them, and it is the totality of the lower world. And even though the element of dust is last, He mentioned it first, and concerning it He speaks. Therefore He began with the earth, which is the point. And the philosophers called it a point because it is below the spheres, set within them like a point within a circle; and they called it a point because it is fixed and established, having no movement at all in its totality, only in its parts, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 1:4): ״והארץ לעולם עומדת״ — and the earth stands forever. And behold, it is the opposite of the uppermost encompassing sphere, for that is supreme in the extremity of fineness and purity, while this is lowest in the extremity of thickness and turbidity. After He began with the earth, He returned to arrange the elements in the proper order, which is fire, wind, and water: He mentioned ״וחשך״ — and darkness — which is the elemental fire, which is dark; and He placed it before wind (רוח) because fire encompasses wind, and He placed wind before water because wind encompasses water, and water encompasses earth. And this very order we find in the book of Ecclesiastes and in the book of Job. And just as He began here from the point, saying ״והארץ היתה תהו ובהו״, so too He began in the book of Ecclesiastes from the point, saying ״והארץ לעולם עומדת״; and afterward He mentioned (Ecclesiastes 1:5) ״וזרח השמש ובא השמש״ — this is the element of fire; and He said afterward ״סובב סובב הולך הרוח״; and He said afterward ״כל הנחלים הולכים אל הים״. And likewise in Job He began from the point as well, saying (Job 28:24) ״כי הוא לקצות הארץ יביט תחת כל השמים יראה״ — this is the element of fire; (Job ibid.) ״לעשות לרוח משקל ומים תכן במדה״ — this is the very order that is here in the book of Genesis. Also in the book of Ecclesiastes, since all three are books of inquiry (מחקר) and their words are founded upon the wisdom of nature. And Scripture informed us here, in saying ״והארץ היתה תהו ובהו״, that the earth put on a form, and the darkness, which is fire, encompasses above, while the water and the dust are mixed together — He called the two of them together תהום (the deep), like the waters of the sea, in which the dust is mixed, and they are called תהום, as it is written (Jonah 2:6): ״תהום יסובבני״. And the wind (רוח) blows and enters within the darkness and hovers over the water. And as for what it says ״ורוח אלהים״ — it should have said ״והרוח״ (and the wind), but He attributed the wind to the name Elohim because of its exceeding fineness; even though fire is finer than wind, since He brought out the fire in the language of darkness, He did not wish to attribute it to the Name, blessed be He." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״בראשית ברא אלהים את השמים ואת הארץ״ — for the merit of three things the world was created: for the merit of חלה (the dough-offering), for the merit of tithes, for the merit of first fruits. ״ראשית״ is none other than חלה, as it is said (Numbers 15:20): ״ראשית עריסותיכם חלה תרימו״; and ״ראשית״ is none other than tithes, as it is said (Deuteronomy 18:4): ״ראשית דגנך״; and ״ראשית״ is none other than first fruits, as it is said (Exodus 23:19): ״ראשית בכורי אדמתך״. And further they said: for the merit of Moses, as it is said (Deuteronomy 33:21): ״וירא ראשית לו״. And all these commandments have one way, and all of them hint at the unity of the Wisdom that is called ראשית; and thus the word בראשית in the Jerusalem Targum is rendered בחוכמתא (with Wisdom). For the commandment of first fruits is a hint at unity, and so too the commandment of the tithe, for the reckoning of ten declares and reveals the power of the One; and so too the חלה is a singular commandment in the dough. And what it says, for the merit of Moses — because we find in him ראשית, and from there he merited to receive the Torah. And if you consider it, you will find that the word בראשית hints at the building of the First Temple, which stood ת״י (410) years, as the numerical value of (Leviticus 16:3): ״בזאת יבא אהרן אל הקדש״. And you already know the saying of the Sages that the Temple was created before the heavens and the earth, and because of this He placed the word בראשית before ברא, and afterward ״את השמים ואת הארץ״. And since it is destined to be destroyed, He said: ״והארץ היתה תהו ובהו״ — because the Shekhinah withdrew from Israel." "<b>And the wind (רוח) of Elohim hovered over the face of the waters.</b> This is the Torah, which we were promised would never be forgotten from the mouth of our seed, even in exile." "<b>And Elohim said: Let there be light.</b> It alludes to the days of the Messiah, concerning which Scripture says (Isaiah 60:1): ״קומי אורי כי בא אורך וכבוד ה׳ עליך זרח״." "<b>And Elohim separated between the light and between the darkness.</b> For in this the Holy One, blessed be He, separates between Israel, who hoped for salvation, and between the idolaters, who dwell in darkness. And to this matter our Rabbis of blessed memory alluded when they demonstrated from this verse the four kingdoms (מלכיות) — that is, they expounded: ״והארץ היתה תהו״ — this is the kingdom of Babylon; ״ובהו״ — this is the kingdom of Media; ״וחשך״ — this is the kingdom of Greece; ״תהום״ — this is the kingdom of Rome; ״ורוח אלהים״ — this is the spirit of the Messiah. And in the explanation of this Midrash, the Holy One, blessed be He, in this passage declares the end from the beginning, and Scripture intended to hint at the end of time within the beginning of time, to teach that the ultimate purpose of creation is the days of the Messiah — and this is: the beginning of thought is the end of deed." "<b>And if</b> you consider further, you will find that in the word בראשית there is the letters of אש ברית; and furthermore, בראשית is two words: ברא שית — these are the שיתין (six hundred) that were created during the six days of Creation; and furthermore, ברא תיש — this is the ram of Isaac, which was created during the six days of Creation; and furthermore, בראשית from the root ארשת, which is speech, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 21:3) ״וארשת שפתיו״ — and its explanation is ב-י' ארשת, that is, by the ten utterances the world was created. <b>Know</b> and understand that the world was created in Tishrei, and the word בראשית alludes to this. And on this matter Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Joshua disagreed in the Gemara: Rabbi Eliezer says, 'In Tishrei the world was created'; Rabbi Joshua says, 'In Nisan the world was created.' And even though Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Joshua — the halakhah is like Rabbi Joshua — they have already decided in the Gemara on this like Rabbi Eliezer, which is what they said: 'According to whom do we pray today, זה היום תחלת מעשיך — according to whom? According to Rabbi Eliezer.'" "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> 'In the beginning Elohim created the heaven and the earth.' Know that all of existence is divided into three parts: the first part is the world of the angels; the second part is the world of the spheres; the third part is this lower world. And the hidden power created all three, and the word בראשית alludes to the hidden power, for it is the beginning of all, a beginning without beginning. And it first created the world of the angels, which are called in the scriptures Elohim, and the world of the spheres, which are the heavens, and the lower world, which is the earth. And because of this matter, it could not have put it first and said 'Elohim created in the beginning'; rather, it was necessary to say 'In the beginning Elohim created,' so that the word ברא would refer back to the word בראשית. And from this you will understand that the hidden power, which is ראשית, created Elohim — and they are the angels — and השמים — and they are the spheres — and את הארץ — and it is the lower world, from the sphere of the moon downward; all of it is called by the name 'earth' collectively. And the verse that comes after it explains this: והארץ היתה תהו ובהו — that is, the earth that it mentioned first, which is the inclusive name, was, at the stage of creation, תהו — that is, matter that received the forms of the four elements alluded to in the verse." "<b>And behold,</b> the term 'earth' is a homonymous term: it is said of the whole and it is also said of the particular — 'and Elohim called the dry land earth.' If so, the word והארץ is a name for the totality of the four elements, and the word תהו ובהו for the element of dust in particular — only that the meaning of תהו ובהו is the element of dust in which there is no existent. And you need to know that the number of the spheres is 955, like the numerical value of השמי\"ם (with a closed מ counted as six hundred). And so too it is found in the Chapters of the Chariot: at the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, descends from the heaven of heavens through 955 firmaments and sits upon His throne of glory in the ארבות, all the gates of the firmament and all the gates of the ארבות say (Psalms 24:7), ״שאו שערים ראשיכם״ and so forth. And so too in Derekh Eretz Rabbah (chapter 2): 'The Holy One, blessed be He, rules in the height, unique, one, and His name is one, and He dwells in 390 firmaments' (like the numerical value of שמים, with the closed מ counted as ordinary); and each and every firmament has His name and epithet inscribed upon it. And they mentioned this name in connection with the verse האזינו השמי\"ם, and there I shall write this midrash, with the help of the Blessed Name. But at the very least, it is impossible to reduce their number, according to the view of our masters the sages of truth, below seven. And this is what they expounded in tractate Hagigah, chapter Ein Dorshin (Hagigah 12b): Rabbi Yehudah says, 'There are two heavens, as it is said (Deuteronomy 10:14), הן לה' אלהיך השמים ושמי השמים.' Reish Lakish said: 'Seven,' and these are they: Vilon, Rakia, Shechakim, Zevul, Ma'on, Makhon, Aravot. And according to the view of the speculative scholars, who are the philosophers, they are nine: the encompassing sphere, second to it the sphere of the constellations, and after it seven spheres of the seven wandering stars, which are שצ\"ם חנכ\"ל. And after these nine spheres, four elements, whose order is: fire, wind, water, dust; and their mnemonic is ארמ\"ע. And Solomon already mentioned them in this order in the first verse, which is what he said (Proverbs 30:4): ״מי עלה שמים וירד מי אסף רוח בחפניו מי צרר מים בשמלה מי הקים כל אפסי ארץ״. According to the philosophers, the element of fire derives its heat from the sphere of the moon, which is above it and adjacent to it, due to the continuous motion of the sphere — for it is well known that every body in motion heats and inflames everything close to it by the persistence of its motion, and all the more so when the moving body is large and powerful, so that it imparts great, mighty, and fierce heat. Therefore the sphere of the moon heats and inflames what is near it, but it itself does not become inflamed or heated, because its matter is pure and refined and is not like the matter of the elements, to receive external actions. And because of this, the element of fire, whose nature is to ascend upward, does not ascend without end, for the surface of the sphere prevents it, its matter being strong like solid crystal; and up to there it ascends along its boundary but does not pass beyond it, just as the surface of the earth prevents the waters, whose nature is to descend downward, from passing through it. And I have already informed you above that the world was created in Tishrei, whose sign is Libra (מאזנים), and so too the following verse indicates, which is what it says: ורוח אלהים מרחפת על פני המים. And it is known that the sign of רוח (wind) is Libra (מאזנים), and the sign of מים (water) is Scorpio (עקרב). And the explanation of the verse is: When did the Holy One, blessed be He, create the heavens and the earth? At the time when the wind of Elohim was sprouting and rising in the morning — that is, at the time when Libra, which is the sign of wind, was hovering over the face of the waters, which is adjacent to Scorpio, which is the sign of water. And the reason for calling them by the names Libra and Scorpio, and likewise the rest of the constellations: stars gathered together in the shapes of Libra and Scorpio; and so at the time of the Flood they gathered in this formation — a cluster of stars in the shape of Aries (טלה), and likewise in the shape of Taurus (שור), and likewise Gemini (תאומים), which is the shape of a man, and likewise the shape of Cancer (סרטן), which is the shape of a beast, and likewise the shape of Libra and Scorpio, and likewise the shape of Aquarius (דלי), which is the shape of a man drawing water. And they were called after these names according to those shapes that appeared in them at the time of the Flood. And it appears reasonable to say that the reason they were seen at the time of the Flood in these shapes was so that we might be roused to the awareness of providence through the indication of the names, as is found among our Rabbis of blessed memory in their midrash." "<b>And you should know</b> also that the molad of Tishrei, in which the world was created, was at בהר\"ד — those are the letters by which the luminaries were created. And how is this known? From the molad of Nisan that comes after it. But since the luminaries were not created until the fourth day, we know with certainty that there never was a בהר\"ד in actuality; rather, it is the true reckoning according to the astronomers." "<b>And that</b> which they instituted to say in prayer — זה היום תחלת מעשיך — it is possible for us to say thus whether according to the sign בהר\"ד or according to the sign וי\"ד. If we reckon according to the sign וי\"ד, this is because man was created on the sixth day after twelve hours of the night had passed and two hours of the day, so that it would be a clear exposition of תחלת מעשיך for judgment. And there is no need to say this according to the sign בהר\"ד, for it would be an exposition of מעשיך referring to the second day, which is the beginning of making, as it is said, ויעש אלהים את הרקיע — for on the first day there was mentioned no making but only creating; and this is the meaning of מעשיך, and it did not say מבראיך." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> In the verse בראשית ברא אלהים את השמים ואת הארץ there is a mystery of the ten sefirot. And the heavens and the earth are the supernal heaven and earth, which are not among the spheres, and they are called שמי קדם and ארץ החיים. And the word בראשית alludes to Ḥokhmah (Wisdom), in the manner of what is written (Psalms 111:10): ״ראשית חכמה״. And likewise the letter bet alludes to Ḥokhmah, which is second among the sefirot. So you have it: in the word בראשית, both the first letter and the word itself testify to Ḥokhmah. And since everything is drawn and emanated from Ḥokhmah, the great bet comes, and even though it is second among the sefirot, it is first with respect to our apprehension. And this is the explanation of the bet of בראשית: חכמה, which is ראשית — for the sefirah preceding it we are not permitted to contemplate, and therefore it is called אין. This is (Job 28:12): ״והחכמה מאין תמצא״ — by way of declaration, not by way of question. Even so, it is alluded to in the point of the bet. And from this the masters of truth expounded, saying to the bet: 'Who created you?' — He showed them by its point, facing the alef, which is Keter." "<b>Know and understand</b> that the Cause of causes is above Keter, and this is the statement of Sefer Yetzirah: 'Ten sefirot בלי מה — ten and not nine, ten and not eleven.' The meaning of 'ten and not nine' is that Keter is to be counted within the totality, since all the other sefirot are drawn from it, for it is a flowing source upon which all depend. 'Ten and not eleven' — because what is above Keter, which is the Cause of causes, is not to be counted within the totality; and it is a hidden thing, of which one may say neither 'is' nor 'is not.' And this is the language of עשר ספירות בלי מה: ten sefirot of בלי מה, that is, emanated from בלי מה. And from this the masters of truth required the point of the yod, which is an allusion to the Cause of causes that is above all ten. And since all ten are the Emanation (Atzilut), behold the Emanator — which is the Cause of causes — is separate from them. This is what is explained: 'ten and not eleven.' And what is said there in Sefer Yetzirah, חקק — and it did not mention who engraved — the intent is upon Keter. For the engraved thing is three sefarim, which are three letters, on account of Keter having engraved them, and they are יה״ו, which are letters that are blessed and bless all that is blessed. And they are also sefer, sefar, and sippur: sefer is Ḥokhmah, and it is called sefer because generations pass and Ḥokhmah endures only through a sefer (book); and from this the supernal Ḥokhmah too is called sefer. Sefar is Binah, in which a person counts (מונה), and it is from the language of sefirah (counting) and minyan (numbering), as Scripture says in Chronicles (II 2:16): ״ויספר שלמה את כל האנשים הגירים אשר בארץ ישראל אחרי הספר אשר ספרם דוד אביו״. Sippur is the rest of the sefirot, which it is possible for human beings to apprehend and to speak of. And know that sefer is the yod; sefar is the he; sippur is the vav, and it is Tiferet, which is the sixth attribute — understand this. And you already know that the numerical value of ספ״ר is ש״ם (340); and if so, three sefarim are three names of the intellect, and even though their powers are many, all three have one root. And likewise the three letters of the Name — behold, they are one root, and they proceeded from one root: He who engraved the world, which is Keter, which is drawn from the Cause of causes, called Ein Sof. And you should reflect on the section of Marah, in what Scripture says (Exodus 15:25): ״שם שם לו חק ומשפט ושם נסהו״." "<b>Elohim.</b> This is Teshuvah, and it — i.e. the Name Elohim — created the world, and to it is directed our prayer on Rosh HaShanah. And what is written (Genesis 20:17): ״ויתפלל אברהם אל האלהים״ — is said concerning the final he. And so said David, blessed be his memory (Psalms 55:17): ״אני אל אלהים אקרא״. And concerning Teshuvah it is said (Proverbs 2:3): ״כי אם לבינה תקרא״ — and Teshuvah is called Binah, for it is set among the five preceding sefiros. ״אקרא״ and ״תקרא״ are language of prayer, in the language of (Genesis 13:4): ״ויקרא שם אברם״, which Onkelos translates וצלי. את ואת — to include Gedullah (Greatness) and Gevurah (Might). השמים — that is Tiferet, and it is Eden, and the two pillars called Neẓaḥ and Hod are included in the word השמים, that is, the heavens and its two pillars; for since we find no act of creation for the pillars of heaven, in any case we must say they were included within the generality of השמים. Also the righteous one is included with them, for he is a pillar through which the world is sustained. Or one may say that the vav of ואת comes to include these three pillars. והארץ — that is the Garden (Gan), which receives from Eden. <b>And the earth was</b> — He now mentions what it was at first, and it is called by the name ארץ because it is the end of the structure. And He said היתה — that is, the essence of its existence was from that existence which was, and it is the Ḥokhmah that preceded everything. <b>Unformed (תהו)</b> — that is Binah, which is the green line (קו ירוק) that encompasses the entire world. <b>And void (ובהו)</b> — that which instructs about something beginning to take shape, and it is Gedullah. <b>And darkness (וחשך)</b> — that is Gevurah. And furthermore, in this verse there is an allusion to how many are the days of the world, and it is very clear from what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: 'Six thousand years is the world.' And the discerning one will understand. Therefore it immediately mentioned והארץ היתה תהו ובהו — to allude to one that will be destroyed (חרוב). Also in the first word of the Name of forty-two (מ״ב) that issues from it, you will find the same alluded to: that the world created in six will be uprooted and destroyed — and this is clear." "<b>Know</b> that what the verse did not explain regarding the heavens — what they were at first, as it explained regarding the earth — from the fact that it explained the matter of the earth, you can understand the matter of the heavens. And upon this it relied and was silent: it did not mention the matter of the heavens, nor did it explain what the spheres were before they reached their culmination as they are today. But according to the Kabbalah that I have alluded to you, the matter will come true, established, firm, and enduring — that it did not explain the matter of the heavens because they are Eden, and how could one explain what it was at first? And the Sages, of blessed memory, already expounded (Isaiah 64:3): ״עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך״ — this is Eden, which no eye has ever seen. And therefore He revealed its matter and concealed the hidden things, and elaborated upon the revealed. And understand this well: the heavens and the earth in the first verse are Tiferet and the Crown (Atarah), and they are Eden and the Garden (Gan), which the verse mentions before it. And this is the midrash of the Sages, of blessed memory, that Gan Eden was created before the world was created; and if the Garden was created, how much more so Eden, for the Garden is planted in it — as it is written (Genesis 2:8): ״ויטע ה׳ אלהים גן בעדן מקדם״." "<b>And you should know</b> that we have in our hands a tradition regarding this verse of בראשית, that from it issues the Name of forty-two (מ״ב), attributed to the attribute of Judgment (Din), up to the bet of בהו — but by means of many permutations (צרופים). And if you illuminate the eyes of the heart, you will find in it the count of בהר״ד that I mentioned above, and it is inscribed in the parashah, and its four letters are found, between each and every letter, among the forty-two letters. And the discerning one will understand — he will be able to distinguish that this is not a matter of chance, but it is a pure proof for the creation of the world ex nihilo, for before mountains were born and there was no בהר״ד, the attribute of the world's Judgment was in place of בהר״ד, acting without instrument and bringing forth instrument for His work. And this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {10 "The citation of Job 28:12 reads 'מאין תמצא' as 'from Ayin (Nothingness) it is found,' reading declaratively rather than as the question 'where shall it be found?' — this is the deliberate Kabbalistic reading, preserved as written.", 11 "Citation of II Chronicles normalized as II 2:16 following the Hebrew chapter-verse; the gematria ספ״ר = ש״ם = 340 is copied exactly as written.", 12 "The heading 'Elohim' is formatted as a lemma though the Hebrew shows no bold markup in the source; it is rendered consistently with the commentary's pattern. The phrase 'והבינה נקראת תשובה' underlies the opening identification, here expressed in Rabbeinu Bahya's tersely elliptical manner. The section blends Kabbalistic sefirotic readings into the verse lemmas; all preserved as written.", 13 "The verse citation (Isaiah 64:3) follows the standard numbering; in some Masoretic traditions this corresponds to 64:4, but the citation as written is preserved without adjustment.", 14 "בהר״ד is the Hebrew year-date (creation-reckoning) alluded to; preserved exactly as written without expansion of the acronym."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:23:45.324950865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}